1.1. 
===================uml class diagram
    Class Diagrams: The most frequently used structural diagram in C++, depicting classes, their member variables (attributes), member functions (methods), and relationships like inheritance and composition.
    //------------- https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/system-design/unified-modeling-language-uml-class-diagrams/
    Sequence Diagrams: A behavioral diagram used to visualize how objects in a C++ system interact over time, typically showing the order of function calls.
    Package Diagrams: Used to organize C++ code into logical groups, reflecting namespaces or physical file structures.
    State Machine Diagrams: Crucial for systems with complex internal states, such as finite state machines (FSM) commonly found in embedded C++. 


===================Tools for Generating UML from C++ Code
Because manually maintaining diagrams is difficult, many developers use "reverse engineering" tools to generate diagrams directly from existing header and source files: 

    clang-uml: An automatic generator that uses Clang to produce up-to-date class, sequence, and package diagrams in formats like PlantUML and MermaidJS.
    Visual Paradigm: Supports round-trip engineering, allowing you to generate ANSI C++ code from diagrams and reverse engineer UML from existing code.
    Doxygen: While primarily for documentation, it can generate inheritance and collaboration diagrams using Graphviz.
    Software Ideas Modeler: Provides features to transform existing C++ source code into various UML diagram types.
    hpp2plantuml: A focused tool specifically for converting C++ header files into PlantUML syntax. 


